          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-49b1c079187b0c98\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Marguerite directly and retrospectively avows that she loved Armand immediately, comparing that love with her attachment to her dog. The utterance presents a loving emotional state as true. It does not itself undertake a commitment, and its reflective placement within an extended explanation provides no independent evidence of expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The avowal also reassures and explains Marguerite’s conduct, but those conversational effects do not give it performative undertaking force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Marguerite addresses Armand directly within represented dialogue: « je t’ai aimé tout de suite autant que mon chien ».\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Marguerite explains why Armand’s pity moved her and why she gave herself to him unusually quickly. Her comparison to the only being she says she had previously loved stresses the exceptional sincerity and emotional significance of her attachment. The surrounding discussion also indicates that Armand’s later jealous letter damaged that love. This explanatory and retrospective framing strongly favors a truth-conditional avowal rather than a new promise or reflexive outburst.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« Eh bien, je t'ai aimé tout de suite autant que mon chien. »\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person statement, with “tout de suite,” presents Marguerite’s loving state and its immediate onset as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Marguerite explains that Armand took her hand, wept, and was the only human being willing to pity her before making the highlighted statement.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance occurs as a considered causal explanation of her attachment, not as an affect-driven verbal reflex; this supports high T and E=0.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She subsequently refers to « l'amour que j'avais pour toi » and says Armand’s letter harmed it.\",\"supports\":\"This continuation independently treats her love as an emotional state that existed and was later diminished, reinforcing the truth-conditional reading.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as affirmative French perfect tense in Chapter XV.\",\"supports\":\"The supplied grammatical metadata is consistent with a retrospective avowal rather than a prospective undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core force as a retrospective avowal of love. The unusual comparison, explanatory purpose, and relational consequences belong to content and context rather than exposing a gap in T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
